.TH KITTY 3
.SH NAME
kitty \- serial terminal graphics and input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -b '#κ' /dev

.B /dev/kbd
.B /dev/kittyctl
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I kitty
device uses a dedicated byte stream to display the kernel screen
in a terminal supporting the Kitty graphics protocol.
It provides a software framebuffer for the ordinary
.IR draw (3)
device, including the software cursor.
The PC adapter also displays the bitmap console;
the ARM64 text console remains on the PL011 UART.
Mouse reports are delivered through
.IR mouse (3).
The device is currently included in the
.BR pc ,
.B pc64
and ARM64
.B qemu
kernels.
Rebuild and install
.B /sys/src/libflate
for the target architecture before linking these kernels.
.PP
Set
.BI kittyuart= port
and
.B monitor=kitty
in the kernel configuration.
The UART is selected by its driver name or its
.B eia
number and must not be the serial console or already open.
Names must identify exactly one UART.
It is reserved until reboot and configured for eight data bits,
one stop bit, no parity, and no flow control.
Physical UARTs run at 115200 baud.
Ordinary reads and writes to its
.B eia
and
.B eiactl
files are refused.
No port is opened and no screen is changed without
.BR kittyuart .
.PP
The terminal and the UART must pass bytes unmodified, without
local echo, line processing, or injected console messages.
QEMU can connect the serial port directly to a terminal, socket,
or pseudoterminal.
The checkout's
.B boot/qemu
launcher selects a modern virtio-serial generic port for graphics with
.BR -kitty ;
Console output defaults to
.B .qemu.console.log
in the exported root.
The graphics stream is selected by the name
.BR virtserialport1 ,
independently of architecture and UART enumeration.
On x86 COM2 is connected to a null backend;
on ARM64 the PL011 remains the console.
For example, from a Kitty-compatible terminal with a built kernel
and userland:
.IP
.EX
boot/qemu -amd64 -kitty
boot/qemu -arm64 -kitty
.EE
.PP
The launcher probes hardware acceleration when the host and guest
architectures are compatible:
KVM on Linux, Hypervisor.framework on macOS, WHPX on Windows,
and NVMM on NetBSD.
It initializes a paused machine to check that the accelerator and
required CPU and interrupt-controller configuration are usable,
then reports the selected backend before booting the guest.
If the probe fails, or the architectures differ, it uses TCG software emulation.
RISC-V uses TCG because its current raw kernel boot requires the
space reserved by OpenSBI below the kernel.
.PP
For HiDPI scaling, use
.B -x
with
.B -kitty
or
.BR -kitty-uart .
An optional number from 0 to 8 selects the scaling mode:
.IP
.EX
boot/qemu -amd64 -kitty -x 2
.EE
.PP
The corresponding kernel configuration parameter is
.BI kittyscale= n
with a default of 0, meaning no scaling.
Bare
.B -x
or
.B -x 1
selects an automatic factor of 2 when terminal cells are at least
28 pixels tall, and 1 otherwise.
This choice is reevaluated when terminal geometry changes.
.B -x 0
disables scaling;
.B -x 2
through
.B -x 8
selects an explicit factor.
The number can also be attached to the option, as in
.BR -x2 .
The logical Plan 9 screen is the terminal's pixel area divided by
the selected factor,
rounded down.
Each logical pixel, including those of the software cursor, is
expanded by that factor in both directions using nearest-neighbour replication,
not terminal image interpolation.
Mouse coordinates are divided by the same factor.
Fonts, borders, and menus therefore become larger without changing
Plan 9 applications.
An explicit factor is fixed at boot; terminal resizing changes the
logical screen dimensions, not that factor.
.PP
The
.B uartvcon
transport in the
.BR pc ,
.B pc64
and ARM64
.B qemu
kernels supports only generic port 1 of a modern PCI virtio-serial
controller with memory-mapped capabilities and the multiport feature.
The launcher supplies this controller and a
.B virtserialport
connected directly to terminal standard input and output.
It does not use
.BR virtconsole ,
which may discard output under backpressure.
The transport has eight 8192-byte receive buffers and one
8192-byte transmit buffer.
Completed receive buffers are retained until the input queue has room.
Baud settings do not limit virtual transport speed.
There is no modem signalling, serial-console polling, port discovery,
or hotplug support.
Closing the UART resets the entire controller before another open;
do not attach other ports to it.
Closing can discard the last transmit buffer if the host has not consumed it.
.PP
.B -kitty-uart
instead connects COM2 to terminal standard input and output and
selects UART 1, as needed by kernels without
.BR uartvcon .
This option is only supported on x86;
ARM64 must use
.BR -kitty .
For a physical connection, configure
.B kittyuart
with the appropriate UART name or number instead of using the launcher.
.PP
.B -kitty
and
.B -kitty-uart
cannot be combined with
.B -vga
or
.BR -cpuwait .
RISC-V screen support is not provided.
If the launcher is killed before it restores terminal modes,
run the host's terminal reset command.
Rebooting the guest ends QEMU; otherwise stop QEMU from another
host terminal.
.PP
At startup the driver enters the alternate screen, negotiates
graphics and keyboard support, and requests the terminal's
pixel and character-cell dimensions.
Before geometry is reported, the logical screen is derived from
an 800 by 600 terminal pixel area and the configured scale,
with a minimum of 128 pixels per dimension.
Automatic scaling initially uses a factor of one.
Geometry reports resize the logical framebuffer, up to 1920 by 1200 pixels,
and its scaled output, up to 3840 by 2400 terminal pixels.
Dimensions smaller than 128 logical pixels are ignored.
The output dimensions are the logical dimensions multiplied by the scale;
rounding down can leave a strip smaller than one scaled pixel at the edges.
Cell dimensions are limited to 128 pixels.
Resizing is also polled for terminals without in-band notifications.
Draw clients receive the usual mouse resize notification.
Do not reconfigure this screen using
.IR vga (3).
.PP
Dirty, cell-aligned tiles are copied out of the framebuffer,
converted to RGB, and transmitted in bounded inline base64 chunks.
Each tile is compressed with level-1 zlib only if that reduces its size.
Incompressible tiles are sent uncompressed.
Encoding, serial writes, and acknowledgement waits occur in a
separate kernel process, never in a drawing or interrupt callback.
By default up to eight distinct tiles are sent before waiting for one
terminal status response.
Damage is coalesced while the terminal is slow.
After five seconds without an acknowledgement, transmission stops.
.B Kittyctl
reports the connection status, screen and cell dimensions,
and transmission and input counters.
The screen dimensions are logical pixels;
.B scale
gives the current scale factor and
.B output
gives the covered area in terminal pixels.
It also reports the compression setting, compressed tile count,
raw and transmitted pixel payload bytes, batch limit, and batch count.
Writing
.B redraw
restarts negotiation and requests a full repaint.
Writing
.B compress 0
or
.B compress 1
disables or enables compression.
Writing
.B batch
followed by a number from 1 to 16 sets the maximum tiles per acknowledgement.
These settings apply to subsequent transmissions;
write
.B redraw
to repaint an unchanged screen with the new settings.
.PP
.B Kbd
is an exclusive-reader stream of complete, NUL-terminated keyboard
messages understood by
.IR kbdfs (8).
The boot script binds it before starting
.B kbdfs
so the usual
.B /dev/cons
and
.B /dev/kbd
interfaces work without a new user-space decoder.
Kitty keyboard press, repeat, release, modifier, and associated-text
reports are accepted, with legacy escape-sequence fallback.
SGR pixel mouse reports, cell-coordinate fallback,
focus changes, and bracketed paste are supported.
Paste is streamed rather than accumulated; control characters
other than tab and newline are discarded.
Input sequences and pending key state have fixed size limits.
Input exceeding the queue limit is dropped and counted.
